{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '92,111p;148,192p' docs/02-product-spec.md && cat reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/data.prompt.md && rg -n '1\\\\.5|1\\\\.6' docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md && git status --short\"","aggregated_output":"| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| F1 | Bar and area value axes always include zero. An author `domain` that excludes zero is widened and raises AX110. | Property test on domains, including all-negative and mixed-sign data; `bars` fixture (values 82–97) starts at 0 | R1 |\n| F2 | Missing values are never drawn as zero and never bridged unless the author asks. An explicit `null` is a gap, silently. An isolated value between two gaps is a visible point. A missing bar shows a \"no data\" mark at the baseline, distinct from a zero bar. On a time or date axis with a regular step, an absent step is a gap and raises AX105; `missing: 'zero'` on the field turns absent and null values into zeros for counts. Tooltip, table and announcements say \"No data\". | `trend` with nulls and `trend` with the same 7 rows deleted render the same gap; `dirty` fixtures; property test that no segment connects across a missing index | R5 |\n| F3 | Input that cannot be plotted as given is reported, never guessed. The diagnostic registry (section 3.5) fixes each case's level, what is drawn and what the reader sees. | One test per code; the combined `dirty` fixture and the four one-bad-value fixtures yield exactly the codes listed in section 3.5 | R5, D5 |\n| F4 | Three temporal types, never mixed silently. **Calendar dates** (`2026-02-24`, `2026-02`, `2026`) read the same in every viewer time zone. **Instants** (epoch milliseconds declared `type: 'time'`, `Date` objects, ISO strings with an offset) are shown in `timeZone` (default: the viewer's), with the zone named in the tooltip heading and the description. **Wall-clock times** (date-time strings without an offset) are shown as written, with hint AX112. Instants that all fall on the same time of day, whole days apart, raise hint AX111 (\"these look like calendar dates\"). A bare number is never assumed to be an instant. | Four fixture variants in UTC, America/Los_Angeles and Asia/Kolkata: (a) date strings: identical text in all three; (b) ISO strings with `Z`, (c) epoch ms with `type: 'time'`, (d) `Date` objects: each zone's correct local text, zone visible, AX111 raised; with `timeZone: 'UTC'` or `type: 'date'`: identical. Plus: both DST transitions, a leap day, an unparseable date | R3 |\n| F5 | No smoothing. When a line is denser than the screen, the drawing is simplified with this guarantee: for every device-pixel column, and every contiguous run of valid samples inside it, the drawn stroke covers exactly that run's minimum and maximum. A break between two runs is always kept, even when both fall in one pixel column; a slot a series merely has no row for (weekly data beside daily) does not break its line. Stacked areas are simplified on their cumulative sums. Exact values always come from the data, not the drawing. | Assertions on which source samples the drawing kept, for: one outlier in 1,000,000; two peaks and a gap in one column; alternating valid and missing samples; tied extremes; device pixel ratio 1 and 2; several zoom levels. A sample that the drawing dropped is still reachable by keyboard and in the table | D8, D9 |\n| F6 | Numbers and dates are formatted for the page locale. Ticks and readouts are formatted separately: ticks may be compact (`50K`); tooltip, table and announcements show the full integer part, and the fraction with up to six significant digits not counting the fraction's leading zeros (`123,456.75`; `0.000123456`; `0.1 + 0.2` reads `0.3`), with the unit. A custom formatter that throws falls back to the default and raises a diagnostic. | Expected strings for chosen values (large integers, small decimals, percentages, zoned times) in `en-US`, `de-DE` and `en-IN` | R4 |\n| F7 | Domains follow one order of precedence: the zoomed view is inside the author's `domain` if given, otherwise inside the automatic domain. The automatic domain contains all data, reference lines and stack totals, and ends at the first tick at or beyond each extreme. A field declared `scale: 'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1) ends at full scale only when its maximum is at least half of full scale and not above it. Nothing is inferred from a label. | Property tests; `bars` ends at 100 when declared `percent`; a 4% maximum ends at the first tick at or above 4% | R1 |\n| F8 | Donut: slices keep the given order, start at 12 o'clock and run clockwise. The legend shows every slice's value and percentage as text, in slice order. A zero slice is listed without an arc; a `null` slice is listed as \"No data\". A negative value is AX120 and the chart shows its error state. With more than eight slices, the seven largest are drawn and the rest are grouped as \"Other (n)\", with AX121; the table lists every category. An all-zero donut shows the empty state. | `parts` fixture; one test per case | R7, landscape scoreboard 3.1 |\n| F9 | Up to eight series, no two share a color. Beyond eight, each series still has a unique combination of color and dash or marker, and AX130 is raised. | Tests with 6, 8 and 12 series | R7 |\n| F10 | Stacks place positive and negative values on their own sides of zero, and the axis covers both totals. A missing component is drawn with zero height and the stack is marked \"incomplete\" in tooltip, table and announcements. | Property test on stacked geometry; mixed-sign and incomplete fixtures | R5 |\n| F11 | Line, area and scatter are drawn in x order whatever the input order. On line, area and bar, two rows with the same x in one series are AX108 and that series is not drawn. On scatter, points sharing an x (or both coordinates) are ordinary data. | Shuffled `trend` renders identically; duplicate fixture | R5 |\n| F12 | When a warning means values were left out or changed, the chart says so to the reader: a short note (\"2 values not shown. See table.\") inside the figure and in the accessibility tree, in every build. | Production build given `dirty` shows the note | R5, R6 |\n\n### 3.2 Legibility\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L1 | No visible text overlaps other text or is clipped, and no label disappears without the reader being told. Remedies, in order: thin (time, date, numeric and `ordered` category axes only); wrap to two lines; rotate up to 45°; truncate in the middle, keeping every label distinct, with the full text in tooltip and table. If labels still do not fit, a visible note (\"Some labels hidden. See table.\") appears and AX143 suggests horizontal bars. Axylo never changes a chart's orientation on its own. | In real browsers, after fonts load: for widths 240–1,920 px and generated label sets, zero intersecting text boxes (1 px tolerance), zero text outside the figure, truncated labels pairwise distinct. Repeated with the WCAG 1.4.12 text-spacing overrides, 200% text-only zoom, and a font that loads late | R2 |\n### 3.5 Diagnostic registry\n\nLevel decides delivery. **Error**: the chart or a series cannot be drawn; the chart shows its error state. **Warning**: something is not drawn as given; the reader sees the note in F12. **Hint**: advice for the author. Errors and warnings print once per code per page in every build; hints print in development builds only. `strict: true` throws on errors and warnings found while validating a create, `update` or `append` call, synchronously from that call. A diagnostic that can only arise later (a contrast check after the page's CSS changes, a custom formatter that throws when a table page is built, a label that stops fitting after a resize) is reported when it is found and is never thrown. Every diagnostic names the chart; those about data name the field and the first row indexes in the caller's own data.\n\n| Code | Level | Condition | What is drawn |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| AX100 | Error | Typed columns have different lengths | Error state; the message names the columns and their lengths |\n| AX101 | Error | A named field is not a key of any row | Error state; the message lists the keys that exist and the closest match |\n| AX102 | Warning | A field is absent (`undefined`) in some rows | Gaps |\n| AX103 | Warning | A value is `NaN` or infinite | Gap |\n| AX104 | Hint | Every non-missing value of a value field is a plain decimal string (`'40'`, `'-1.5'`) | Parsed and drawn; the message suggests `parse: 'number'`. An empty string counts as missing |\n| AX105 | Hint | Steps are absent on a regular time, date, wall-clock or number axis | Gaps. When a series would need more absent steps than twice its observations (and more than 1,000), it is treated as irregular instead: no gaps are created and the hint says so |\n| AX106 | Warning | A value field mixes numbers with strings, or holds strings that are not plain decimals (`'1,234'`, `'n/a'`) | The strings are not drawn |\n| AX107 | Warning | A date or time cannot be parsed | That row is not drawn |\n| AX108 | Error | Duplicate x within a series, or duplicate donut labels | That series, or the donut, is not drawn |\n| AX109 | Hint | Numbers on an axis declared `time` look like seconds, or a numeric x looks like epoch milliseconds without `type: 'time'` | As declared |\n| AX110 | Warning | An author domain on bar or area excluded zero | Domain widened to zero |\n| AX111 | Hint | Instants look like calendar dates | As instants, zone shown |\n| AX112 | Hint | Date-time strings have no offset | As written |\n| AX120 | Error | Negative donut value | Error state |\n| AX121 | Warning | More than eight donut slices | Seven largest plus \"Other (n)\" |\n| AX130 | Warning | More than eight series | All series, with dash or marker differences |\n| AX140 | Warning | No `title` and no `labelledBy` | Generated name |\n| AX141 | Warning | Palette below 3:1 on the resolved background | As themed |\n| AX142 | Hint | Plot too small to draw | Summary and table control |\n| AX143 | Hint | Labels hidden after every remedy | Note shown to the reader |\n| AX150 | Warning | Unknown option key | Ignored; the message suggests the closest known key |\n| AX151 | Warning | `append` rows out of order | Accepted through the slower full update |\n| AX131 | Hint | A compact chart has more than one series | Drawn, with dash differences |\n| AX132 | Hint | More bars than pixels along the category axis | Drawn; the message suggests aggregating |\n| AX133 | Hint | A scatter chart has more than 50,000 points | Drawn; the message says drawing may be slow |\n| AX134 | Error | Series × x positions exceeds 20,000,000 (long rows with many series and little shared x) | Error state; the message gives both counts and suggests fewer series or a scatter chart |\n| AX152 | Warning | `timeZone` is not a zone the browser knows | The viewer's zone is used |\n| AX160 | Warning | A custom formatter threw | The default format is used for that value |\n\nExpected results for the Phase 1 fixtures: the combined `dirty` fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`) yields AX102, AX103 and AX106 once each, draws Mon–Tue as a segment and Sun as a point, and shows the F12 note. `dirty-string` (one `'40'` among numbers) yields AX106. A column made only of numeric strings yields AX104 and is drawn in full. `dirty-null` yields nothing.\n\nThe split between AX104 and AX106 answers a reviewer's objection that common back ends return counts as strings (Postgres `bigint` and `numeric`, BigQuery): a whole column of plain decimal strings is unambiguous and is parsed; a stray string among numbers is a bug and is not.\n\n### 3.6 Type matrix\n\n| | Line | Area | Bar | Donut | Scatter |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Picking by pointer | Nearest x | Nearest x | The band under the pointer | The slice under the pointer | Nearest point within 24 px; ties go to the later row |\n| Tooltip content | Every visible series at that x | Same, plus the stack total | Every series in that category | Label, value, percentage | The point's series, x and y; coincident points listed, up to eight |\nYou are implementing one module of Axylo, a browser charting library, in this repository. Other agents are working in the same checkout on other directories at the same time.\n\nFirst read `AGENTS.md` and follow it exactly. Then read:\n- `packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts`: the fixed interfaces. You produce `Frame` (AlignedFrame, PointFrame, DonutFrame), use the SLOT_* states and the join rule, and report through `DiagnosticSink` with `DiagnosticInput` (note its `effect` and `origin` fields).\n- `packages/axylo/src/public.ts`: the public options you must accept (Data, Encoding, ValueField, XField, DonutOptions, ScatterOptions, CommonOptions, Diagnostic).\n- `docs/02-product-spec.md`: criteria F2, F3, F4, F8, F10, F11, F12, DX2, DX3, DX4, and the diagnostic registry in section 3.5 (every AX code, its level, its condition and what is drawn). The expected results for the dirty fixtures are stated under the registry.\n- `docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md` sections 1.2 (the three data forms and their shared rules), 1.3 (fields), 1.5 (commit contract: streaming rules) and 1.6 (the wording of diagnostic messages).\n\n## You own (create and edit only these)\n- `packages/axylo/src/data/**`\n- `packages/axylo/src/diagnostics/**`\n- `packages/axylo/test/data/**`\n- `packages/axylo/test/diagnostics/**`\n\n## What to build\n\nPure TypeScript that runs in Node. No DOM. No runtime dependencies.\n\n1. **Diagnostics** (`diagnostics/`).\n   - A registry: every code in spec 3.5 with its level and a message builder that produces text of the form \"what, where, how to fix\" (see plan 1.6 for the tone and an example). Messages name the field and give the first offending rows.\n   - A collector implementing `DiagnosticSink`: merges by code, field and effect, adds counts, caps example rows at 10, keeps insertion order, and can return the list as public `Diagnostic` objects once given the chart's name.\n   - A helper that computes the reader's disclosure from a diagnostic list: how many values were omitted and how many were changed (only effects 'omitted' and 'changed' count).\n\n2. **Options resolution** (`data/options.ts`). Turn the public options of each chart type into one internal, fully explicit description: which field is x, which value fields there are and whether the data is long (one value field plus an optional series field) or wide (several value fields), each field's label, unit, scale, missing policy, parse flag, declared type and time zone. Reject what the types cannot catch at run time: an unknown option key raises AX150 and suggests the closest known key (edit distance); a field named in the options that is not a key of any row (or not a column) raises AX101, lists the keys that exist and suggests the closest.\n\n3. **Normalization** (`data/normalize.ts` and helpers). `normalize(chartType, resolvedOptions, environment, sink, epochAndRevision) → Frame`.\n   - Accept the three forms: long rows, wide rows, typed columns (`{ columns }`). Typed columns are borrowed: use the caller's y array directly when the data is already in ascending x order; never mutate it. Columns of different lengths raise AX100.\n   - **x kind** (spec F4): strings `YYYY-MM-DD`, `YYYY-MM` and `YYYY` are calendar dates (`date`, position = that date at 00:00 UTC, with `dateUnit` = the precision written, the finest if mixed); ISO strings with `Z` or an offset and `Date` objects are instants (`time`); ISO date-times without an offset are wall-clock (`wall`, position = the written fields read as UTC, hint AX112); numbers are `number` unless the field is declared `type: 'time'` (then epoch milliseconds); other strings are `category` (position = index in first-seen order). A declared `type` wins over inference. A value that cannot be parsed as the column's kind raises AX107 and that row is not placed. Raise the hints AX109 (numbers declared `time` that look like seconds, or an undeclared numeric x that looks like epoch milliseconds) and AX111 (instants that all share one time of day and are whole days apart, within one hour for daylight saving: \"these look like calendar dates\"). Resolve `timeZone: 'local'` to `environment.timeZone`.\n   - **Values**: a finite number is a value; `null` is SLOT_NULL; an empty string is SLOT_NULL; an absent key or `undefined` is SLOT_ABSENT_FIELD (AX102); `NaN` or an infinity in a row is SLOT_INVALID (AX103); in a typed column `NaN` is SLOT_NULL with no diagnostic. Strings: when every non-missing value of the field is a plain decimal string (optional sign, digits, optional fraction, optional exponent; nothing else, no thousands separators), parse the column and raise the hint AX104 once (no hint when the field says `parse: 'number'`); otherwise each string is SLOT_INVALID and AX106 is raised.\n   - **Alignment** (aligned frames): the frame's x column holds every distinct x of any series, ascending (category: first-seen order). Each series has a y and a slot state for every x. Rows are sorted by x whatever the input order (spec F11). Two rows with the same x in one series raise AX108 with effect 'series-disabled'; the series keeps the first occurrence and gets `drawable: false`.\n   - **Slots without a row.** For each series on a `time`, `date` or `number` axis, infer its own step: take the differences between its consecutive observed x positions (for `date`, in units of its `dateUnit`; for `time`, treat differences within one hour of a whole number of days as whole days); if the series has at least three observations, the smallest difference is the step when every difference is a whole multiple of it and at least half of them equal it. A slot that lies on that series' step grid and has no row is SLOT_GAP (an expected step is absent: raise AX105 with the count and the first positions); if no other series has that x either, insert the x position so the gap exists as a slot. Any other slot without a row is SLOT_NO_ROW. On a category axis a slot without a row is SLOT_NO_ROW.\n   - **`missing: 'zero'`**: as described in the contracts next to the SLOT constants.\n   - **Scatter** (`PointFrame`): per series, x and y sorted by x then y, keeping the source row of each point; a point with a missing or invalid x or y is left out and reported with the same codes.\n   - **Donut** (`DonutFrame`): labels and values in the given order. A negative value raises AX120 with effect 'chart-error'. Duplicate labels raise AX108 with effect 'chart-error'. Zero and null are kept (the view decides what to draw).\n   - Every diagnostic you raise has `origin: 'call'` and the right `effect` ('omitted' when a value is left out of the drawing, 'changed' when it is drawn differently from what was given, 'none' for hints that change nothing).\n   - Every slot and point keeps the index of the caller's row it came from; `Frame.source.rowAt(i)` returns the caller's row (for typed columns, an object built on demand).\n\n4. **Streaming store** (`data/store.ts`). A store that owns growable buffers for an aligned frame and supports `append(rows, { keep })` per plan 1.5: it returns a new frame with a higher revision and the same epoch, shares buffers with the previous frame (the new frame is a view onto the same buffers plus the appended part; amortized O(appended) work, no full copy), and with `keep` drops the oldest distinct x values beyond the limit. Rows before the last x are accepted by renormalizing everything and raise AX151. Appending to typed-column data and to wide and long rows must all work. Diagnostics for appended rows use the same rules.\n\n5. **`validateOptions`** (`data/validate.ts`): runs options resolution and normalization for a chart type and returns the diagnostics, with no DOM (this backs the public `validate()`).\n\n## Tests (required)\n\nExpected values come from the spec, not from running your code and copying its output.\n- One test per diagnostic code you raise, asserting the code, level, effect, field, example rows and count, and that the message names the field and the fix.\n- The dirty fixtures exactly as the spec states their results (combined, and one bad value at a time: null, NaN, a string, undefined).\n- A column of only numeric strings is parsed with AX104; with `parse: 'number'` there is no hint.\n- A misspelled field raises AX101 and suggests the right key.\n- F4: the same series as (a) date strings, (b) ISO strings with `Z`, (c) epoch milliseconds declared `time`, (d) `Date` objects: (a) gives kind `date`; (b), (c), (d) give kind `time`, raise AX111, and resolve the zone from the environment; an ISO date-time without an offset gives `wall` and AX112. Include a daily series across the March and November 2026 daylight-saving changes in America/Los_Angeles (stored as instants at local midnight) and check that AX105 is not raised for it, and a leap day.\n- F2: a daily series with seven explicit nulls and the same series with those seven rows deleted both produce seven slots that may not be joined across (SLOT_NULL in one, SLOT_GAP with AX105 in the other). A weekly series next to a daily series is SLOT_NO_ROW between its observations and raises nothing.\n- F11: a shuffled input gives the same frame as the sorted input. Duplicate x raises AX108 and marks the series not drawable.\n- DX2: the same data as long rows, wide rows and typed columns gives equal x positions, equal values and equal slot states (with the documented difference that NaN in a typed column means missing), and diagnostics that point at each form's own row indexes.\n- fast-check properties: x positions are strictly ascending; every y is NaN exactly where the slot is not a value; `row` indexes are valid; normalizing twice gives equal frames; appending row by row gives the same frame contents as normalizing all rows at once; with `keep = k` the frame never holds more than k distinct observed x values and always holds the latest ones.\n- A million-point typed-column normalization finishes in well under a second on this machine and does not copy the y column when x is already ascending (assert buffer identity).\n\nRun `npm run typecheck` and `npm test` from the repository root before you finish. If something outside your directories fails, say so and leave it alone.\n\nFinish with the report described in AGENTS.md.\n107:### 1.5 Handle and commit contract\n130:### 1.6 Diagnostics\n299:- React adapter prototype, used to test the commit contract (1.5, 1.7).\n M packages/axylo/src/layout/index.ts\n M packages/axylo/src/layout/labels.ts\n M packages/axylo/src/simplify/index.ts\n M packages/axylo/test/layout/layout.test.ts\n M packages/axylo/test/simplify/simplify.test.ts\n M reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.events.jsonl\n M reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/view-scene.events.jsonl\n?? reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/data.fix.events.jsonl\n?? reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/data.fix.report.md\n?? reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/layout.fix.events.jsonl\n?? reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/render.review.events.jsonl\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
